WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.680
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:12.180
Yes, that’s right, Microsoft’s new AI watched&nbsp;
about a 100 million clips from Youtube. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:18.480
today we are going to ask one simple question:&nbsp;
what did it learn? I’ll tell you in a moment.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:26.400
You see, today text to image AIs are all the rage.&nbsp;
By just doing as little as entering a text prompt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:32.460
we can now create all kinds of images, and&nbsp;
even videos. A true miracle of science,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:37.680
really. And I often hear people saying that&nbsp;
they can’t wait to be able to be able to express&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:44.760
their creativity by creating movies with them.&nbsp;
However, not so fast. We can already do that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:50.460
with Google’s and Runway’s technique and there&nbsp;
are even more out there. But for some reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:57.060
I rarely hear people talking about sound.&nbsp;
Well, this new paper solves exactly that.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:02.280
Now, wait a minute. We are going to&nbsp;
invoke the Second Law of Papers here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:06.180
which says that whatever you are&nbsp;
thinking about, there is already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:11.520
a Two Minute Papers episode on that. For&nbsp;
instance, we talked about Google’s MusicLM,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:19.920
an incredible AI-based technique that takes a text&nbsp;
prompt and synthesizes incredible music. Listen.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:42.420
This is fantastic. Also, our seasoned Fellow&nbsp;
Scholars remember an earlier episode where we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:48.180
talked about a brilliant handcrafted graphics&nbsp;
paper that did something that was previously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:54.420
thought to be almost impossible. So what&nbsp;
is that? Well, hold on to your papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:59.340
because it took an already existing&nbsp;
video clip or computer animation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:04.740
and synthesized sounds for it.&nbsp;
And the results were outstanding.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:09.360
Yes, through the power of computer&nbsp;
graphics research, this was possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:15.780
approximately 10 years ago. Yes, you heard&nbsp;
it right. This paper was published 10 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:20.700
ago at the SIGGRAPH conference. That&nbsp;
is an incredible place with similarly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:26.880
high-quality papers. Now, with all that said,&nbsp;
what does this new AI paper add to the mix?

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:32.880
And I would say, everything. Now, I hear&nbsp;
you asking, Doctor, what do you mean by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:40.320
this? Exactly what I said. This is an any to any&nbsp;
generation technique that also includes audio.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:46.020
Here are some of my favorite examples.&nbsp;
One, if we enter a piece of text,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:52.440
like “Fireworks in the sky”, it synthesizes&nbsp;
video and audio at the same time!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:59.100
Or, we can write "Dive in&nbsp;
coral reef”, and we get this.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:07.440
Two, we can also go the other way around. If we&nbsp;
have a text description of a painterly style,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:22.020
and look, no mention of ships at all. And we&nbsp;
also add this sound sample. And as a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:25.020
we get this image. So cool!

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:32.400
But wait, it gets even better. Or three, we can&nbsp;
even enter a text prompt plus a piece of audio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:39.780
and out comes, not just an image, but…oh&nbsp;
my, even a piece of video. As requested,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:45.000
the camera indeed goes forward ever&nbsp;
so slightly. Good job, little AI!

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
And, we can also perform the usual suspects, text&nbsp;
to image, a piece of audio to an image, or if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:59.160
have a cracking photo, we can even ask for it&nbsp;
to create a video with a somewhat similar vibe.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:05.760
Wow. It feels like this can do absolutely&nbsp;
everything. Now, every single one of you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:11.580
Fellow Scholars see that the quality of these&nbsp;
results is not the greatest, however. Remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:18.960
